module std where

-- =========================================================================
-- Enumerable instance bodies — the typed successor enumeration over the
-- numeric tower (solver-extension Phase 1). These are the `enumFromTo` method
-- values the `Enumerable Int` / `Enumerable Nat` instances reference
-- (dispatch-instances.fenel), and the typed stepper the solver de-fence
-- (`engine:enumerableSet`) spends to turn an integer-typed `WsInterval` into a
-- real finite witness set. [[project_numeric_tower]]
--
-- WHY THIS UNFENCES THE SOLVER. `enumerate.fenel`'s `enumerateSet` returns
-- Nothing for `WsInterval` — the markers carry NO base type, so integer-stepping
-- would be UNJUSTIFIABLE (the P5 "NO integer-stepping invented here" rule). The
-- base type, recovered from the SetExpr's TYPE context (fork (A)), names which
-- `Enumerable` instance steps it; `intEnumFromTo` IS that step for ℤ. It is NOT
-- "inventing" integer-ness — it is the typed instance the PROVIDED base selects.
--
-- THE PURE CONS-SPINE. `enumFromTo` returns the pure-ADT `List a` view
-- (`Cons`/`Nil`, the μX. Nil | Cons head:a tail:X form), NOT the native array
-- repr — the conceptual definition of a bounded range as a successor fold. The
-- de-fence folds this spine into the array-repr witness list the WitSet readers
-- expect (`intListToNumberArray`, enumerate.fenel). [[feedback_opaque_is_optimization]]
--
-- ENGINE-BUG WATCH (the numeric-tower §0 hazards, none re-tripped here):
--   §0.2 (Succ/mkInt-built × DEEP arithmetic recursion blows the stack) — the
--     step UNIT is built via `numberToInt 1` (the bridge path), not a `Succ`/
--     `mkInt` literal, and `intAdd`/`intLt` are shallow per step (no natMul/
--     natGcd recursion under them); the recursion is on the `hi − lo` range,
--     bounded by the interval width.
--   §0.3 (a bare uppercase nullary ctor in ARGUMENT position parses as a var)
--     — `Nil` appears only in RETURN/then position (the arrayToList precedent,
--     safe); `Cons lo (…)` parenthesizes its recursive tail; `(Succ lo)` is
--     parenthesized in arg position. No bare nullary ctor in an arg slot.
--   TOTALITY: `intEnumFromTo` recurses on the shrinking `hi − lo` Nat measure
--     ⇒ the totality checker returns Unknown (the natDiv / natGcd precedent) —
--     total over the intended bounded-interval domain, NOT `@partial`.
--
-- NEW standalone std fnIds (`std:intEnumFromTo` / `std:natEnumFromTo`); NOT
-- added to stdNatives (the D1 shadow trap), NOT given a defineFunction TS spec.

@label "Int enumFromTo"
@description "The `Enumerable Int` step: the bounded integer range [lo..hi] inclusive as a pure cons-spine `List Int`. intEnumFromTo lo hi = if intLt hi lo then Nil else Cons lo (intEnumFromTo (intAdd lo (numberToInt 1)) hi). The step unit is built via numberToInt 1 (the bridge path, not a Succ-literal — the numeric-tower §0.2 stack hazard); intAdd/intLt compose the Phase-2 Int bodies. lo > hi ⇒ Nil (empty range); lo == hi ⇒ a singleton. Total over the bounded-interval domain (recurses on the shrinking hi − lo measure; the totality checker returns Unknown — the natDiv precedent)."
@tags "numeric" "int" "enumerable" "solver"
intEnumFromTo : (lo: Int) -> (hi: Int) -> List Int
intEnumFromTo lo hi =
  if intLt hi lo then Nil
  else Cons lo (intEnumFromTo (intAdd lo (numberToInt 1)) hi)

@label "Nat enumFromTo"
@description "The `Enumerable Nat` step: the bounded Peano range [lo..hi] inclusive as a pure cons-spine `List Nat`, stepping by Succ. natEnumFromTo lo hi = if natLt hi lo then Nil else Cons lo (natEnumFromTo (Succ lo) hi). Pure Peano successor stepping (no bridge needed — Succ is the Nat successor; natLt is shallow/structural per step, NOT the natMul recursion the §0.2 hazard names). lo > hi ⇒ Nil. Total over the bounded range (recurses on the shrinking hi − lo measure; Unknown to the totality checker)."
@tags "numeric" "nat" "enumerable" "solver"
natEnumFromTo : (lo: Nat) -> (hi: Nat) -> List Nat
natEnumFromTo lo hi =
  if natLt hi lo then Nil
  else Cons lo (natEnumFromTo (Succ lo) hi)

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Enumerable Char (solver-extension Phase 2) — codepoint stepping over the
-- `Char` opaque rep. Char is NOT numeric, but it IS a discrete ORDER (the
-- Unicode codespace), so it has a successor: the next codepoint. The stepper
-- COMPOSES the existing host leaves `charCode` (Char → codepoint Number) +
-- `charFromCode` (Number → Char) — NO new host leaf (the no-TS gate; charCode/
-- charFromCode are the ledgered `Char` Reprable lift/lower). This generalises
-- `Enumerable` past the numeric tower: a Char-typed `WsInterval` (`c >= 'a' and
-- c <= 'e'`) now enumerates to `['a','b','c','d','e']`. [[feedback_opaque_is_optimization]]
--
-- ENGINE-BUG §0.2 — Char's pure form is a refined Nat, but its REP is
-- number-direct (codepoint, NOT a materialised Peano spine — the foundation.fenel
-- note), so stepping via `charFromCode (add (charCode lo) 1)` does NOT build a
-- `Succ`-spine and does NOT trip the deep-recursion stack hazard. §0.3 — `Nil`
-- rides return position, `Cons`/`(charFromCode …)` parenthesised.
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label "Char enumFromTo"
@description "The `Enumerable Char` step: the bounded codepoint range [lo..hi] inclusive as a pure cons-spine `List Char`, stepping by the next Unicode codepoint. charEnumFromTo lo hi = if gt (charCode lo) (charCode hi) then Nil else Cons lo (charEnumFromTo (charFromCode (add (charCode lo) 1)) hi). Composes the host leaves charCode/charFromCode (NO new leaf); the order is by codepoint (charCode), exactly as charLt orders Chars. lo > hi (by codepoint) ⇒ Nil; lo == hi ⇒ singleton. Total over the bounded range (recurses on the shrinking codepoint gap; the totality checker returns Unknown — the intEnumFromTo precedent)."
@tags "char" "enumerable" "solver"
charEnumFromTo : (lo: Char) -> (hi: Char) -> List Char
charEnumFromTo lo hi =
  if gt (charCode lo) (charCode hi) then Nil
  else Cons lo (charEnumFromTo (charFromCode (add (charCode lo) 1)) hi)
